itƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx*https://www.nber.org/papers/w35471#fromrssƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;3tjNmJa8HrYSWHDYHjP1zUTzbOF76ohvfir9cQvZM18ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Kzaewywe26Ul0Xkt4YSqbyJ07UVp0cJ-FnbxgFUif6Eƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YxCCXv2tVOkmRMOLSAw759Ohu3BFBhbLYZtLIT621-Uƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx*https://www.nber.org/papers/w35471#fromrssƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zVclmlo16o_toGTkirL9MwM_RBWEz_LUCSLIMCCd5Nkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyMany previous studies have used cross-cohort comparisons and a focus on in utero exposure to document the lingering effects of the 1918 influenza pandemic on survivorsâ€™ outcomes later in life. A subset has examined impacts on old-age health and mortality, finding mixed results. This paper contributes to the literature by extending the analysis to evaluate early-life and childhood exposure to the pandemic on old-age longevity along with a set of candidate mechanisms. We employ Social Security Administration death records linked to the full-count 1940 census and employ event-study and difference-in-difference regressions to compare longevity of cohorts with different age-at-exposure who were born in cities with high versus low exposure based on 1918 influenza-related mortality. Our treatment-on-treated calculations suggest that cohorts with age-at-exposure of 3-8 and 9-11 experience 6.4 and 11.2 months reductions in old age longevity; importantly, we note that these cohorts are often used as â€œcontrolsâ€ in the broader literature. While our main analysis focuses on males due to data constraints, supplementary results suggest smaller and less precise effects for females. An analysis of census data suggests reductions in health, education and socioeconomic scores as potential mechanisms.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;cTrvzcnswqcAqtMPoAWzIRngmL3F594lBQZzbMRIsOQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;70dGExIzPM2t3ewe9e4rH_fjdZCYC5pr-q2oQenJnUQƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;g5E-2L120ZNlPtyCCWDMoIszqpzgPfERklMrqRT3ZOcƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Wr8M-D39WbpFCRkOYbHZoofE9I_Xpu8G2z-gRDN2dG0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hzZR7M3Pfl-YYdqFK5xYDpjedcn76KAA8S2WP5Zg8Fgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;7eJPR_g7-T1i7BJrev3FiTcmita55wOna31s3nfZAHkƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;oR_lLZ4QYk8Cyc1Z8S9tEKah5OwqD5E7Q83hpZktiwcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IFBKGpC27tu2f_If479HoPjUQoZIhYiMnHaCJlcs4mkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;VwzIWdwe50tYIeHbe8uQQU3sEdr-pVKO3bPAsr2xvWUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx(urn:rmn:predicate:source-reports-summaryƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;xHb